<div class="page">
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p>This publication discusses two sets of rules that may limit the losses that you can deduct on your tax return from any trade, business, or income-producing activity. The first part of the publication contains the passive activity rules. The second part discusses the at-risk rules. However, when you figure your allowable losses from any activity, you must apply the at-risk rules before the passive activity rules.</p>

  <h2>Useful Items</h2>
  <p>You may want to see:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><span data-field="link_to_rental_property">{{ link_to_rental_property }}</span></li>
    <li><span data-field="link_to_tax_information">{{ link_to_tax_information }}</span></li>
    <li><span data-field="link_to_s_corporations">{{ link_to_s_corporations }}</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Form (and Instructions)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><span data-field="form_investment_expense">{{ form_investment_expense }}</span></li>
    <li><span data-field="form_passive_activity_loss_limitations">{{ form_passive_activity_loss_limitations }}</span></li>
    <li><span data-field="form_passive_activity_credit_limitations">{{ form_passive_activity_credit_limitations }}</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Passive Activity Limits</h2>
  <p>Generally, you are in a passive activity if you engage in an activity that is not materially participating in the activity. These terms are extensive, and you have a lot to learn.</p>

  <h3>Passive Activity Rules</h3>
  <p>You can take passive activity credits only from tax on passive activity. Passive activity credits include net losses from passive activities, net business credits, such as the credit for fuel produced from a nonconventional source. Credits in excess of the tax on income from passive activities are carried forward to the next tax year.</p>

  <h3>Who Must Use These Rules</h3>
  <p>The passive activity rules apply to:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Individuals.</li>
    <li>Estates.</li>
    <li>Trusts (other than grantor trusts).</li>
    <li>Personal service corporations.</li>
    <li>Closely held corporations.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Grouping Your Activities</h3>
  <p>You may treat one or more trade or business activities as a single activity if those activities form an appropriate economic unit for gaining loss or using the passive activity rules.</p>

  <h3>Appropriate Economic Units</h3>
  <p>Generally, to determine if more than one activity forms an appropriate economic unit, you must look at the relationship between the activities and the economic factors that have the greatest weight in determining whether activities are treated as an appropriate economic unit.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <p>Page 2</p>
  <h3>Personal Service Corporations</h3>
  <p>For the purposes of passive activity rules, a personal service corporation is a personal service corporation if it meets all of the following requirements:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>It is not an S corporation.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Activities That Are Not Passive Activities</h3>
  <p>The following are not passive activities:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Trade or business activities in which you materially participate.</li>
    <li>Any activity in which you are an investor in an activity.</li>
    <li>Any activity in which you materially participate.</li>
    <li>Any activity in which you are an investor in an activity.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Rental Activities</h3>
  <p>In general, a rental activity is not a passive activity. However, you may group them together if the activities form an appropriate economic unit.</p>

  <h3>Example.</h3>
  <p>Herbert and Wilma are married and file a joint return. They own a rental property. Herbert is the sole shareholder. Wilma is a partner. Together, they own a rental property. Herbert and Wilma's property is treated as a single rental activity. Herbert and Wilma's property is treated as a single rental activity.</p>

  <h3>Passive Activity Credits</h3>
  <p>You can take passive activity credits only from tax on passive activity. Passive activity credits include net losses from passive activities, net business credits, such as the credit for fuel produced from a nonconventional source. Credits in excess of the tax on income from passive activities are carried forward to the next tax year.</p>

  <h3>Passive Activity Rules</h3>
  <p>You can take passive activity credits only from tax on passive activity. Passive activity credits include net losses from passive activities, net business credits, such as the credit for fuel produced from a nonconventional source. Credits in excess of the tax on income from passive activities are carried forward to the next tax year.</p>

  <h3>Example.</h3>
  <p>Example. John Jackson has a significant interest in a bakery and a grocery store. He owns a bakery and a grocery store. He owns a bakery and a grocery store. He owns a bakery and a grocery store. He owns a bakery and a grocery store.</p>

  <h3>Passive Activity Rules</h3>
  <p>Passive activity rules are designed to limit the amount of losses that you can deduct on your tax return from any trade, business, or income-producing activity. The first part of the publication contains the passive activity rules. The second part discusses the at-risk rules. However, when you figure your allowable losses from any activity, you must apply the at-risk rules before the passive activity rules.</p>
</div>

